{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Implementation landed as Gerrit change 2087 (`453ad3e40de`, Change-Id\n`I7b0fe78fe3080ce37ee4ba67d55ef7a369cc9025`). Recording the RED/GREEN sequence and the mutation\nevidence the acceptance criteria call for.\n\n## RED/GREEN, not test-after\n1. `tests/unit/test_janitor_concern_schema.py` was written FIRST and run against the untouched\n   tree: 3 passed. The invariant it asserts (11 numbered concerns == \"eleven\") held then.\n2. Concern #12 was added to `discovery.md` WITHOUT touching the schema sentence.\n   `test_schema_concern_count_matches_the_concern_list` went RED with exactly the intended\n   diagnosis: `AssertionError: ... lists 12 numbered concerns (highest: 12) but its output schema\n   says the subagent must pick 'which of the 11 concerns above'` / `assert 11 == 12`. Not an\n   ImportError, not a collection error — the assertion itself failed on the real drift.\n3. The schema sentence was then updated to \"twelve\" and the suite went GREEN.\n\nThat is the actual bug this file guards, reproduced before the fix and closed by it.\n\n## Mutation evidence (each mutation applied to `discovery.md`, then reverted)\n| # | Mutation | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Rewrote every `^N. **` concern heading as `- **` (numbering stripped) | `test_discovery_defines_a_numbered_concern_list` FAILED — the anti-vacuous guard fires rather than the suite passing on an empty list |\n| 2 | Renumbered `12. **Oracle strength` as `13.` | `test_concern_numbers_are_contiguous_from_one` FAILED (`[1..11, 13]` vs the expected contiguous run) |\n| 3 | Reverted the schema word `twelve` -> `eleven` | `test_schema_concern_count_matches_the_concern_list` FAILED (`assert 11 == 12`) |\n| 4 | Removed the count clause entirely (`which of the twelve concerns above` -> `the concern this belongs to`) | FAILED in `_schema_count` — \"no 'which of the <N> concerns above' schema sentence\" |\n\nEvery test in the file is killed by at least one mutation, and each mutation kills a DIFFERENT\ntest, so none of the three is redundant and none is a tautology. Mutations 1-4 all edit the live\nfile the tests read (`DISCOVERY.read_text()`), not a docstring or a comment.\n\n## What was deliberately NOT kept\nAn earlier draft carried `test_number_word_table_is_self_consistent`, parametrized over the\nmodule's own `NUMBER_WORDS` constant. It asserted test data against a literal range — it exercised\nno artifact and could not fail on any real change. Given this ticket is about oracle strength, a\ntautology in its own gate would be self-refuting, so it was deleted before the commit rather than\ncarried as filler coverage.\n\n## Why this is not a change-detector\nBoth sides of the comparison are DERIVED from the document at run time: the concern count from\n`^(\\d+)\\. \\*\\*` matches, the stated count from the number word in the schema sentence. Rewording a\nconcern's title, reordering the bullets inside a concern, or rewriting the prose of any concern\nleaves all three tests green. Only a genuine disagreement between the two halves — or the loss of\none half — fails them. It survives every behaviour-preserving edit to the artifact it guards.\n\n## Frontmatter arithmetic, verified rather than asserted\nThe `SKILL.md` description measured 980 characters before the edit and 997 after inserting\n`oracle strength, ` (17 characters) into the discovery topic list, against the 1024-character cap\nat `scripts/check_skill_frontmatter.py:46`. Measured by parsing the YAML frontmatter, not by\ncounting the source line. `python scripts/check_skill_frontmatter.py` reports\n`PASS: 4 SKILL.md file(s)`.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\nAll 23 criterion commands were executed verbatim from the repo root against the post-change tree\nand all pass; each had been executed against the pre-change tree first and failed there, so none is\nvacuously satisfied. `git diff --name-only origin/main` lists exactly the five Scope paths.\n`make lint`, `make typecheck` and `python scripts/check_complexity_baseline.py --check`\n(`new=0 increased=0`) are clean.\n\n## One correction found while running the criteria\nThe pre-remediation AC extracted the portable core with\n`awk '/^## Oracle strength .../,/^## /'`. That range is a no-op: awk evaluates the end pattern\nagainst the same record as the start, and `^## ` matches the heading line itself, so the range\ncollapsed to one line and all seven greps failed on a correct implementation. Replaced with\n`sed -n '/^## Oracle strength & contract coverage (concern 12)/,/^## Temporal decay/p'`, which\nextracts the full 32-line section. Caught by running the criterion rather than reading it."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787383655376420001,"uuid":"9aff9bf2-414d-4850-8a55-a8d2842810f6"}